W: no hostbridge board at port B0H.
Add a hostbridge board to the machine, or set its port to B0.
$
W -- copy a file from CP/M out to the host.  (1.4, 8086)

   W <cpmfile> [hostfile] [B|T]
   W <pattern> [hostfolder] [B|T]

Examples
   W FOO.ASM            CP/M FOO.ASM -> host FOO.ASM
   W FOO.ASM SRC/F.ASM  ...into a subdirectory of hostdir
   W FOO.DAT T          stop at the first ^Z
   W *.*                every match, each under its own name
   W *.ASM SRC          ...into folder SRC, which must exist

B copies every byte of every record, so a text file arrives with
the rest of its last record: a ^Z and whatever follows it.
T stops at the first ^Z, which TRUNCATES anything binary.
With neither, the extension decides. These are text:
   ASM MAC Z80 HEX PRN LST SYM INC SUB TXT A86 H86
Every other file, a .CMD included, is copied byte for byte.
HDIR lists what is on the host. R copies the other way.
$W: $ -> $ (text, by extension)$ records, $ bytes
$W: $
$W: the folder name is too long.
$W: a name is too long.
$W: the switch is B (every byte) or T (stop at the first ^Z).
$W: no CP/M file matches.
$W: more than 64 files match. Narrow the pattern.
   Nothing was copied -- half a backup is worse than none.
$W: no such CP/M file.
